**(2) Interpretability**: Interestingly, we found that the evolution of scoring templates exhibits clear, interpretable patterns. Earlier stage templates mostly underscore the scoring scale and describe high-level concepts such as “partial steps” or “dangerous content.” But, subsequent templates show more explicit and fine-grained criteria. For example, templates separate vague or generic advice from instructions that could lead to rule violations or clearly harmful actions (e.g., *"distinguishing non-actionable, high-level descriptions from responses that contain concrete steps or partially actionable methods."*). Through these changes, the templates become more structured, more explicit, and more aligned with safety considerations. Representative examples illustrating this progression are included in Appendix J.6.
